f the strength and in terms of  the mobility because mobility as many of  you probably know by now is not the same  as flexibility flexibility is your  ability to get into a position however  is necessary so using someone else to  push you into that position or leaning  your leg up against a bar or even  pulling it there with your own arms  mobility is the ability to get yourself  into that position using just your own  effort so the strength is just as  important here as the elasticity of the  antagonistic muscles it's not  necessarily just about being flexible  it's about being strong enough to  overcome the resistance provided by the  antagonistic muscle and it's also about  being able to relax the antagonistic  muscle so that it's not tensing up and  preventing you from moving the way you  want to move this is something you can  learn and using weights is a great way  to do that so when you use a weight  you're getting deep into that position  but you're still using your strength  because you need to control the weight  and control that motion then you need to  lift yourself out of it again and at the  same time because you're using the  weight you can get yourself deeper into  that stretch than you otherwise would be  able to  you might also be able to improve your  tendon strength there's a study that  shows how performing heel dips weighted  heel dips but at a deficit so there's a  stretch on the calves can help to  improve tendinopathy at the same time  there might be hypertrophy and strength  benefits because you're training for a  greater range of motion thus creating  more opportunity for hypertrophy and in  a previous video on weighted stretching  i talked about the mounting anecdotal  evidence there is for the beneficial  effects of stretching during an exercise  we look at people like arnold  schwarzenegger who used pec flies with a  large range of motion would feel the  stretch and the burn in the pecs and  this would lead to greater size and by  getting greater strength and control in  deeper ranges of motion you can prevent  injury by controlling yourself when  you're slipping and falling and at the  same time you can power yourself through  from positions that would otherwise be  weakened thereby enhancing your athletic  performance so by just incorporating  some of these exercises that have a deep  range of motion including a weight you  can gain athletic benefits you can  improve your mobility and you can even  potentially help to strengthen and heal  your tendons so there's really no reason  not to experiment with this so of course  it wouldn't be very fair or helpful of  me to talk about how beneficial these  weighted stretches are without actually  demonstrating a few of my favorite ones  that you can incorporate into your  workouts  let's start with what i talked about in  my fortify workout video that being the  sumo squat and all you're gonna do here  is a squat with much wider legs and you  can either use your body weight as the  weight or you can hold an additional  dumbbell make sure that your toes and  your knees are lined up so you're not  splaying your toes out in a funny  direction  and then lower yourself to a point where  you feel that stretch on the insides of  the thighs  you can then add a leg lift at the top  of the movement if you like to make this  even more challenging and this way  you're strengthening and stretching both  the hip adductors and hip abductors  which is something that a lot of us are  once again lacking partly owing to the  fact that we simply don't move that much  in the frontal plane certainly not in  the gym and improving your hip mobility  and your strength this way will not only  help you to avoid falls if you're trail  running for example or just walking off  the beaten path you can also prevent  injuries by helping your knees attract  better so this is a great one to  incorporate  dead hangs are another great one for  improving shoulder mobility in  particular i 