 a counterbalance so if you are a  bit back heavy it will then bring you  back forwards of course you can also use  the pistol squat by holding onto a wall  or a rope to balance or you can put one  foot up onto something higher which  makes the mobility challenge a little  bit easier and of course you can get  closer and closer to the ground over  time  then of course there's the cossack squat  which i'm a huge fan of we're  essentially doing a kind of side lunge  but heading all the way down keeping  your heel flat on the ground once again  and this trains all kinds of mobility as  well as strength and is a fantastic way  to build up to the pistol squat  the hamstring mobility one of the best  things you can do is the good morning  and here you're going to have the weight  on your shoulders maybe slightly lower  down then you're going to hinge at the  hips keeping the weight over the midline  of the foot you can start with just a  bar even you want to keep this very very  light and this of course will get a nice  stretch on the hamstrings once again  more than you can probably easily  achieve on your own but at the same time  it's also a great exercise for  strengthening the lower back and  teaching you the hip hinge movement  obviously you have things like the  jefferson curl which are fantastic for  this kind of pike mobility however if  we're looking at exercises that provide  other benefits and that aren't just all  about the mobility then i think that  this one is particularly effective and  it's something that a lot more people  are going to feel comfortable doing  also commonly missing for a lot of  people is thoracic mobility in the mid  spine if you're looking for an exercise  that can train this while at the same  time providing a bunch of other benefits  then look no further than the hindu  push-up in the hindu push-up you start  off in a kind of downward dog position  dive bomb towards the ground to a lower  press up position and then you lift your  chest up trying to extend the spine and  really focusing on making sure that  you're extending at the at the sternum  or just below the shoulder blades rather  than the lower back then you can return  to that starting position again if you  try and keep your heels mostly on the  ground at the same time then you'll also  get a nice hamstring and calf stretch in  here as well and of course you're also  getting all the benefits of a push-up  and another one that's great for  overhead shoulder mobility for  stretching the pecs and lats is the  dumbbell pullover which is also just a  great exercise for building the pecs and  lats i'm going to demonstrate you here  in the worst possible way  so that's the split squat which ben  patrick is a big fan of it's a fantastic  exercise not only for stretching the hip  flexors which is something that's  extremely tight on  ninety percent of desk workers but it's  also great for ankle mobility because  you're leaning forward so much at the  same time you're getting the benefits of  a squat it's great for building stronger  legs through a massive range of motion  and just watching ben patrick perform as  a testament to how powerful this  movement can be  he can dunk and all sorts of things but  you've also managed to get the scissor  split without stretching for it  specifically  and for the carves what you can do is a  car phrase but starting with a hill much  lower than the ball of your foot whether  you put it on a wedge or just pile up  some plates so you can feel the stretch  in the calf before you begin this is  also a great way to get stubborn calves  to grow  most of us already have the strength we  need to lift pretty much everything that  we lift regularly in our day-to-day  lives however if you're very tight in  your hamstrings if you've got tight  rounded shoulders and if you've got  tight hip flexors this creates all these  kind of compensatory movement patterns  and all these odd postures and now when  you try and exert that strength at an  awkward angle you can end up hurting  yourself because something is far more  likely to tear or pull if you don't have  that little bit of slack that little bit  of leeway to allow yourself to move in a  comfortable and free way i think so many  people could benefit from focusing on  this a little bit instead of just  thinking hey i want to get into shape  i'm just going to pile on lots and lots  of weight  so my goal at the moment in my training  is to improve my own mobility whilst  maintaining my strength and when you  start to do this you feel so much more  free you're able to move so much more  freely and you realize that a lot of  what you thought of is kind of tiredness  or fatigue it's actually just tightness  caused by sitting in the same position  all day and moving incorrectly once you  gain that mobility again once you relax  those muscles you feel liberated because  you're able to move so much more freely  you feel so much more energetic and you  can express that strength through a  greater range of motion i hope you found  this video useful interesting guys if  you did then please leave a like and  share around it helps me out so much and  i massively appreciate it if you like  this kind of training that looks not  only at strength and aesthetics but also  your overall performance and how you  move in the real world then you might  enjoy my ebook and training program  super functional training there's a  discount on right now and there's a link  in the description down below there you  can also find a link to my physical book  functional training and beyond which is  available for more goods bookstores  including amazon and that's an  introduction to functional training and  how it affects not only athletes but  everybody else so check it out if you're  interested either way thank you so much  for watching this one subscribe if you  want to see more like it hit the bell  button for notifications and i'll see  you next time  bye for 